WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:38.820
So that’s how Cambridge got founded right?

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:43.970
Well, not quite, for example not all students
and faculty left to Cambridge from Oxford,

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:48.480
a large portion of them went to the much closer
town of Reading where they hoped to wait out

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.970
until the University of Oxford reopened.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:55.970
So that begs the question of why did some
students and faculty decide to relocate all

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:57.670
the way east to Cambridge?

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:02.110
Well, turns out not even the geniuses that
came out of the University of Cambridge for

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:05.979
the past 8 centuries could figure out a definitive
answer to this question.

00:01:05.979 --> 00:01:10.280
However, there are a couple of details that
may shine some light on this.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:14.280
For one Cambridge was a well established town
in England gaining its charter in the early

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:18.630
12th century, it also was quite wealthy due
to it being the center for trade in the Fenlands

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:23.810
ever since the Viking era and had a legal
monopoly over all waterborne traffic.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:28.150
This combined with the fact that around it
there where numerous religious establishments

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.700
(Ely Cathedral, Crowland Abbey, Barnwell Priory)
which are vital for the creation of a medieval

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:35.250
university, it seems like Cambridge as a town
was a very good candidate for the formation

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.810
of a second English studium generale.

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:42.510
But this still doesn’t explain why the masters
and students from Oxford traveled so far east

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:47.490
when a much closer town of Northampton, was
arguably an even better fertile ground for

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:50.440
the creation of a university than Cmabridge
was.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:54.510
Maybe we have to look at it from the point
that these people leaving Oxford didn’t

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:59.050
want to establish a new university, but if
they didn’t want to establish a new University

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:04.440
and just wait until Oxford was reopened, why
not go to Reading with all the other students

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:08.920
and faculty or any other much closer town
to Oxford than Cambridge.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.950
To answer that we have to look at the people
that actually came to Cambridge from Oxford.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:18.760
Turns out that at least three masters that
taught at Oxford where from Cambridge, the

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:24.390
most notable of which was John Grim a “master
of the scholars” at Oxford, which is a fancy

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:29.390
way of saying that he was the chancellor of
the University of Oxford before the suspension.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:34.540
So it might as well have been that the masters
simply went back home to wait out the suspension

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:39.380
of the University of Oxford, with some students
and other faculty members following them along

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:44.230
as to establish a temporary colony where masters
could continue to teach and students could

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:45.700
continue to learn.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:51.050
Then after the 5 year suspension of Oxford
ended many of the students and faculty returned,

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:52.240
but not all.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.510
Why didn’t everyone return back to Oxford
is another question there isn’t a good answer

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:57.510
to.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:01.840
It is probably a combination of several possible
factors, including better prices for housing

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:06.370
that could be gotten thanks to the contact
of the several masters that were from Cambridge,

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:11.140
the aid given by the local bishop of Ely and
the fact that the university had a high future

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:15.919
possibility for endowment as the city had
a thriving merchant community and a Jewish

00:03:15.919 --> 00:03:18.510
community that could give out potential loans.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:23.070
Plus over the course of the 5 years as masters
taught in the town, the University of Oxford

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:28.670
colony at Cambridge attracted many new students
and faculty from Eastern England as many of

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:32.000
these people didn’t want to travel all the
way to Oxford.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.280
All these factors, resulted in that the masters
and students which decided to stay at Cambridge

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:42.510
after Oxford reopened, quickly established
a growing center of higher education.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:46.840
This center was then boosted in population
by the establishment of the Franciscans house

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:48.410
in 1225.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:53.120
After that, only 6 years later the University
of Cambridge was officially founded receiving

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.850
a royal charter from Henry 3rd, which allowed
the university to officially act as an independent

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:01.190
legal corporation within the town.

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:05.590
Funnily enough even though the University
of Oxford acted as an independent entity for

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:12.980
almost a century now it didn’t receive its
royal charter until 1248, 17 years after Cambridge,

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:17.799
so as royal charters are concerned Cambridge
is technically older than Oxford.

00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:23.180
During the 1250s a new problem started to
emerge not just in Cambridge but also at Oxford.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:28.470
This problem was a tribelistic division of
the student and faculty predicated on geographical

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:29.640
differences.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:34.290
Meaning the northerns hated the southerners
and vice versa, at Cambridge this culminated

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:39.840
in the 1260 riots were Southerners attacked
the Northerners who promptly retaliated.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:44.220
After this the northerners from both universities
left for Northampton where they started to

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:49.470
set up their own University, which by the
way is technically the third ever University

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:50.730
founded in England.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:54.570
But these northerns made a giant mistake,
they involved themselves heavily with the

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:59.340
Second Barons’ war against Henry the 3rd
who besieged Northampton and won.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:04.550
He promptly dissolved the new university and
banned any new establishment of a university

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:10.160
in Northampton ever again which by the way
had to be officially repealed in 2005 so the

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:13.120
current University of Northampton could exist.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:18.230
With this defeat the northern students returned
back to their respective Universities at Cambridge

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:19.230
and Oxford.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:22.990
To avoid something like this ever happening
again Cambridge and Oxford both imposed an

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:28.210
oath that they will not let anyone establish
any new universities in England or let any

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:33.430
masters teach anywhere in england outside
of the two already existing universities.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:37.630
Which is by the way the reason why no new
English University was established until the

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:38.930
19th century.

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:43.450
When it came to student and faculty housing
at Cambridge, early on most housing was provided

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:46.020
by hostels and also some halls.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:50.470
Now yes these where the cheapest accommodation
in the city, but they weren’t as bad as

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:51.660
you’re picturing.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:57.320
Some of the largest hostels had their own
halls, chapels, libraries and even galleries.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:01.970
Plus they were to the dismay of the townspeople
what we would now call rent controlled by

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:06.020
the decree of the king, meaning no price gouging
could happen.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:11.390
Later on just as with Oxford halls these Cambridge
hostels would either die out or evolve into

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:12.880
today's colleges.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:17.600
The life in these early colleges and hostels
wasn’t as peaceful as one may think.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:22.420
Many of these institutions were heavily divided
depending on the subject, meaning one hostel

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:28.330
may have only had arts students/masters while
another had only law students/masters.

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:33.000
These divides resulted in even more tribalism
than already present at the University and

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:37.460
fights amongst different hostels and colleges
was a common occurrence on the streets of

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:38.460
Cambridge.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:43.410
For example in 1500 a short account speaks
of a 2 day battle between a Hostel and the

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:49.110
Christ College, all while St Clement’s Hostel
decided to join in, picking a fight quote

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:52.490
“against all comers”, because why not.

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:58.520
In another similar instance in 1521 the scholars
of Garret Hostel today part of Trinity College

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:03.800
decided with some other hostels to organize
a raid against Gonville Hall today part of

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:05.850
Gonville &amp; Caius College.

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:10.530
During their initial attack they were unable
to penetrate in to the hall and so proceeded

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:15.280
to start a fire at the west gate of the hall,
hoping to burn down the gate.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:19.440
This plan proved effective and they stormed
the Hall where they looted the premises and

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.230
drank all the liquor they could find, whatever
liqueur they were unable to finish they simply

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:25.270
poured out.

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:30.650
The colleges and hostels also had a prevalent
culture of hazing amongst the members.

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:34.310
For example a punishment for breaking the
hostels rules may have involved something

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:35.690
called sconcing.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:39.810
Where the accused would be forced to drink
from a large vessel called the sconce pot

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:45.000
that could have been as large as 2.1 liters
and filled to the brim with beer.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:48.970
However sometimes to mess with the people
the beer would be salted or the pot would

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:51.640
be filled with other kinds of unsavory liquids.

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:56.490
If the accused finished the pot in one go
he wouldn’t have to pay a fine for breaking

00:07:56.490 --> 00:08:01.750
the rules, however if he didn’t, he either
paid the fine or was forced to pay for the

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:03.560
entirety of the drink in the vessel.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:08.280
Even though sconcing was initially used as
a punishment later on it involved in to a

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:14.030
more of a drinking game which is still allegedly
practiced in some university circles today.

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:17.900
Going in to the 14th century there isn’t
that much we know about the university other

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:21.960
then that it expanded in scope and many new
colleges were established.

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:26.280
Up until now outside of the hostels there
was just one college at cambridge and that

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:32.130
was the Peterhouse, but in the 14th century
the number of colleges expanded drastically,

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:36.900
going from only one college in 1320 to 8 colleges
in 1352.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:42.870
This growth would however be stopped just
as in Oxford by the black death and its frequent

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:45.360
resurgences the following decades.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.040
It devastated Cambridge killing the entire
castle side of the river and as much as half

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:52.050
of the towns population.

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:55.550
However that wasn’t the only bad news for
Cambridge during this century.

00:08:55.550 --> 00:09:00.720
The emergence of the Black death and the death
that came with it created many socio economic

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.070
and political changes across England most
of them favoring the lower classes.

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:10.120
Obviously the aristocracy wasn’t happy with
this and tried everything they could to curb

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:15.730
back these changes, in response the Peasants
rebelled all over England, starting what is

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:17.870
now aptly named the Peasants' Revolt.

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:22.980
During this tumultuous time the Cambridge’s
town and gown problem reached its height.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:28.319
In 1381 as the rest of England was rebelling,
the townspeople decided it was time to deal

00:09:28.319 --> 00:09:33.009
with these annoying and pretentious students
and masters of the so called University of

00:09:33.009 --> 00:09:34.009
Cambridge.

00:09:34.009 --> 00:09:37.920
They gathered in a mob, elected leaders and
went on a rampage.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:42.420
First they looted and destroyed a house of
a high ranking master at the University and

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:45.930
then they set their sites on the Corpus Christi
College.

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:50.350
They looted the old court of the college and
burned any books, charters, and endowment

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:51.720
papers they could find.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:56.550
The rioters then turned to assaulting other
properties of the University until they made

00:09:56.550 --> 00:10:01.339
their way to the Great St Mary’s church
where a common chest of the University was

00:10:01.339 --> 00:10:02.339
kept.

00:10:02.339 --> 00:10:06.720
They took the entire contents of the chest
including, papal bulls, university charters

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:08.360
and many other documents.

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:12.819
They did the same with another University
chest kept at the Carmelite friary.

00:10:12.819 --> 00:10:17.529
After two days of pillaging the University
all the documents and books gotten by the

00:10:17.529 --> 00:10:23.220
townspeople were promptly burned in ceremonial
bonfire set up in Market Square.

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:28.110
This rioting along with the entire Peasants'
Revolt was eventually put down, but the damage

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:29.570
was already done.

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:33.850
Many books and documents were destroyed and
much of the university damaged.

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:39.019
In fact it is mainly due to this 1381 riot
that we don’t know much about the early

00:10:39.019 --> 00:10:41.300
history of the University of Cambridge.

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:45.610
Many documents were destroyed and therefore
it is hard to tell what actually happened

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:48.480
at the University before the 14th century.

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:52.920
None the less the townspeople were punished
by the king and the University received many

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:58.480
new rights giving it a lot more control over
the town’s trade, politics and overall economy.

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:07.309
To ensure that no riot in the future can grow
so strong as the 1381 did.

00:11:07.309 --> 00:11:08.600
That is where I will end this video.

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:12.779
Hope you enjoyed my, somewhat continuation
of the Oxford video, I thought it might be

00:11:12.779 --> 00:11:16.980
interesting to look in to the University of
Cambridge history after finding out how interesting

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:18.700
the Oxford History was.

